@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
' '   description:  An audio player lifecycle shown as a composite state vs the flat equivalent (described).
'   usecase:      Contrast a composite (nested) state machine with a flat one; explain when to nest.
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       multimedia
' ============================================================================
title Example State Machine Variant - Composite vs Flat

' COMPOSITE vs FLAT: the same lifecycle can be drawn two ways.
'
' (A) COMPOSITE — group the Playing sub-behaviors (Loading, Buffering,
'     Streaming, Paused) inside one "Playing" state. External transitions
'     target the composite as a whole; the composite manages its own internals.
'
' (B) FLAT — promote every substate to the top level. There are no nests,
'     just one big list of states and transitions.
'
' The two forms are SEMANTICALLY EQUIVALENT when the composite has no
' special entry/exit/history behaviors. Nesting is a STRUCTURING choice.
'
' WHEN TO NEST (composite):
'   - A cluster of states shares common entry/exit/do behaviors.
'   - External callers only care that you are "Playing", not which sub-phase.
'   - You want a HISTORY pseudostate to resume a sub-phase after interruption.
'   - The flat version has so many states that it becomes unreadable.
'
' WHEN TO STAY FLAT:
'   - Few states, or states with no shared behavior.
'   - Every transition is genuinely top-level.
'   - Nesting would add a layer with no behavioral payoff.
'
' BELOW: the COMPOSITE version is rendered. The FLAT equivalent is listed
' in the closing note.

[*] --> Idle
state "Idle" as Idle

state "Playing" as Playing {
  [*] --> Loading
  state "Loading" as Loading
  state "Buffering" as Buffering
  state "Streaming" as Streaming
  state "Paused" as Paused

  Loading --> Buffering : data_ready
  Buffering --> Streaming : buffer_full
  Streaming --> Paused : pause
  Paused --> Streaming : resume
  Streaming --> Buffering : underrun
}

state "Stopped" as Stopped

Idle --> Playing : play(track)
Playing --> Idle : stop
Playing --> Stopped : end_of_stream
Stopped --> Idle : reset
Stopped --> [*]

note over Idle, Stopped
  <<flat variant>>
  The SAME lifecycle with no nesting:
    Idle, Loading, Buffering, Streaming, Paused, Stopped  (6 top-level states)
    Transitions:
      [*] -> Idle
      Idle -> Loading           : play(track)
      Loading -> Buffering      : data_ready
      Buffering -> Streaming    : buffer_full
      Streaming -> Paused       : pause
      Paused -> Streaming       : resume
      Streaming -> Buffering    : underrun
      Streaming -> Stopped      : end_of_stream
      Stopped -> Idle           : reset
      Idle / Stopped -> [*]
  The flat form lists everything explicitly; the composite form lets
  "Playing" own its sub-phases and external callers simply target "Playing".
end note
@enduml
